#{[17592186317088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317088 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2d45990-077c-5ce4-90c0-dbfc23fdd7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa42d7e4-a254-55bd-8ac0-ef1e79e9d268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33a3c9fc-2528-5adc-9b2e-4d84493c2477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317343 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-14x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}+4∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-14x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce2d6842-c273-5f28-8b8b-7bdf00107d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317372 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-17)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -14x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-84(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{84(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b75f000-c288-5650-b319-990a54e2ce6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317390 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425547 #edb/ref 1080 "False" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "057eb7f3-190a-5da9-a784-33d6a0e88a3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425548 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "069197c9-45d7-5029-a771-c125abb39812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425549 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "097c8db1-3ed4-5c62-8384-75b3665d2464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425550 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b6c661f-588e-50fc-bfe9-0af2d5d3381b